course: dictionary
lesson: 571
title: Words from Down Under
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Aussie is a short, friendly word. It is informal, so people use it in relaxed talk, not in formal writing. Aussie can be a noun: an Aussie is a person from Australia. It can also be an adjective, meaning Australian, as in «an Aussie barbecue».
explain: Look at the words around it to work out which job it does. «They laugh till they cry, and Lily starts a list of Aussie words.» Aussie comes before words and describes them, so it works as an adjective here. It tells you the words come from Australia.
explain: Context also shows how a word feels. Aussie sounds warm and friendly, the way people talk to each other, but you would not use it in a formal school report. To check your meaning, swap in Australian and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Aussie is an informal word, so it is used in chatty speech and friendly writing.
fact: As a noun, an Aussie is a person from Australia; the plural is Aussies.
fact: As an adjective, Aussie means Australian, as in «Aussie rules football».
fact: Aussie rules is a kind of football played in Australia.
fact: Aussie words include «arvo» (afternoon), «servo» (petrol station) and «sweet as» (great).
exercise: What is an Aussie? | A kind of bird | A fish that lives in the sea | *A person from Australia | A type of cake
exercise: «In Brisbane, Nanna buys Ava-Rose an ice cream every arvo.» What does arvo mean? | Lunchtime | Morning | Bedtime | *Afternoon
exercise: «Aussie rules» is a kind of what? | *Football | Swimming | Cooking | Singing
test: In «an Aussie barbecue», what does Aussie mean? | Very hot | Very loud | *Australian | Made of wood
test: Maddie says «sweet as» to mean great. What does sweet as mean here? | Very sweet to eat | Cold and wet | Sad and quiet | *Great
exam: «Pack it in» is Aussie slang. What does it mean? | Go to sleep | Pack your bag for a trip | Start a race | *Stop it
